<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Chur</h1><p class="st-meta">1170 CE – 1526 CE (approx.) · capital Chur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alpine church state ruled by the bishops of Chur, one of the oldest sees north of the Alps, whose temporal power was broken by the leagues of Graubünden.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a> is recorded in 451, making it one of the oldest dioceses north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Carolingian and Ottonian kings granted the bishops immunities, tolls and lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Rhaetia</a>, controlling routes to the Alpine passes. In 1170 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Emperor Frederick Barbarossa</a> raised the bishop to the rank of an imperial prince, giving the church a recognised secular principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> governed estates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> valley, the Engadine and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinschgau">Vinschgau</a> from their court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a>, with officials in castles along the pass roads. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romansh">Romansh</a>, German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> dialects were all spoken in their lands. To resist episcopal power, the subjects formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-gods-house">League of God's House</a> in 1367, which later joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grey-league">Grey League</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions">League of the Ten Jurisdictions</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilanz-articles">Ilanz Articles</a> of 1524 and 1526 stripped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of most secular authority and handed it to the communes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a>. The bishops kept a few rights and the title of prince until 1803, and the Catholic diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a> continues today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-chur.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Chur" width="320" height="322"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHE_Gotteshausbund_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1170 CE – 1526 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ortlieb von Brandis, Paul Ziegler</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romansh, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Chur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-chur" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Chur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4904 532 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Chur lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5224 788L5229 694M5224 788L5224 788"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5224 788h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 788h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 788h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chur. <a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-chur">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
